  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1001 PM MDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nDRIER THROUGH MONDAY:\n\nA rather pleasant start to the work week with seasonable \ntemperatures and dry conditions on tap for Monday. This is mainly \ndue to a building high pressure system over the Great Basin.  \n\n\nPRECIPITATION CHANCES RETURN TUESDAY ONWARD:\n\nBy Tuesday, a low pressure system begins trucking its way across the \nGreat Basin and Intermountain West. While this is occurring, a high \npressure system will begin to set itself up over Texas. Both of \nthese factors combined will act to draw Gulf moisture into the \nDesert Southwest. As a result, precipitation chances return to the \nforecast just in time for the first day of fall. PWATs appear to be \n180-200% of normal for a good chunk of the CWA, with the highest \nvalues forecast to be south of I-70. This moisture stays in the area \nfor a majority of the week. Therefore, precipitation chances will \nremain in the forecast for the foreseeable future, especially over \nthe southern mountains. Coverage of showers and storms ebbs and \nflows each day, with some days seeing more widespread chances than \nothers. As more HiRes guidance becomes available, we will have a \nbetter understanding of timing and coverage of precipitation \neach day.",
